QB.add(30,[{"id":30001,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four aspects of pharmacodynamics?","o":["Absorption and distribution of the drug","Modes of action","Interaction with receptors and signalling pathways","Dose-response relationships","Biochemical and physiological effects of drugs"],"e":"Pharmacodynamics – what the drug does to the body – covers modes of action, interaction with receptors and signalling pathways, dose-response relationships, and biochemical and physiological effects. Absorption and distribution belong to pharmacokinetics (what the body does to the drug), covered in more detail in the third term.","w":["Modes of action are listed under pharmacodynamics.","Receptor and pathway interactions are listed.","Dose-response relationships are listed.","Biochemical and physiological effects are listed."],"k":"PD: modes, receptors, dose-response, effects.","rn":[["","Plasma curve: what dominates the rise vs the fall?","Plasma curve: what dominates the rise vs the fall?","Rise: absorption and distribution; Fall: metabolism and elimination"]]},{"id":30002,"t":"Pharmacodynamics and Drug Effects","s":"What does it mean that “drugs don't create anything”?","o":["They only augment or inhibit what is already in the body","They create new receptors that the body did not have before","They create new enzymes once inside the cell","They only act on bacteria, never on human cells","They produce new physiological functions at high doses"],"e":"Drugs use our enzymes, receptors, proteins and cells and only augment or inhibit what is already there – they change the amplitude and speed of existing functions. The second idea was that the body doesn’t like strange compounds and works to remove them.","w":["They act on receptors we already have.","They can increase enzyme transcription, but they create no new targets.","They act on human cells.","Higher doses cause toxicity, not new functions."],"k":"Drugs modulate; they create nothing.","rn":[["","Do drugs create new effects?","Do drugs create new effects?","No; They augment or inhibit what we already have"]]},{"id":30003,"t":"Pharmacodynamics and Drug Effects","s":"Which definition of a side effect is correct?","o":["An undesirable extra effect at a normal dose","A harmful reaction occurring only at a higher than normal dose, e.g. poisoning","The intended biological effect of a drug given at a normal dose","A harmful effect that includes all adverse and toxic effects","A beneficial effect that only occurs with placebo"],"e":"Therapeutic: intended effect. Side effect: undesirable secondary effect occurring in addition to the desired effect, under normal conditions/dose – an umbrella term including adverse effects. Adverse: harmful reaction under normal use. Toxic: deleterious effect after a higher dose.","w":["That is a toxic effect.","That is the therapeutic effect.","The side effect is the umbrella; toxic effects come from a higher dose.","Placebo is a dummy pill."],"k":"Side = umbrella at normal dose.","rn":[["","What is a side effect?","What is a side effect?","Undesirable secondary effect; In addition to the desired therapeutic effect; Under normal conditions / dose"]]},{"id":30004,"t":"Pharmacodynamics and Drug Effects","s":"A patient with severe pain and a cough is given morphine, which also suppresses the cough. How should the cough suppression be classified?","o":["A side effect that is not harmful","An adverse effect, as it is unintended","A toxic effect from the dose","The therapeutic effect of morphine","A placebo effect"],"e":"Not all side effects are harmful. Morphine’s antitussive (cough-stopping) action is a side effect; for a patient with pain and a cough, it does not hurt them. Adverse effects are the harmful part of the side-effect umbrella, e.g. nausea and vomiting with chemotherapy.","w":["Adverse effects are harmful; this one isn’t.","Toxic effects come from a higher dose.","The intended effect is pain relief.","Placebo is a dummy pill."],"k":"Morphine cough suppression = non-harmful side effect.","rn":[["","Opioids: example of a side effect that is not harmful?","Opioids: example of a side effect that is not harmful?","Stopping cough (antitussive)"],["","Chemotherapy nausea and vomiting is an example of what?","Chemotherapy nausea and vomiting is an example of what?","Adverse (harmful) side effect"],["","Are all side effects harmful?","Are all side effects harmful?","No; Adverse effects are the harmful ones"]]},{"id":30005,"t":"Pharmacodynamics and Drug Effects","s":"Nausea and vomiting caused by chemotherapy at a normal dose are best described as:","o":["Adverse effects","Toxic effects, as chemotherapy is a form of drug poisoning","Therapeutic effects","Placebo effects","Effects of chemical antagonism"],"e":"Adverse effects are harmful reactions under normal conditions of use. The lecturer’s example: nausea and vomiting with chemotherapy, which impacts quality of life. Toxic effects follow a higher dose.","w":["The dose is normal, so these are adverse, not toxic.","Not intended.","Not a dummy pill.","Chemical antagonism inactivates an agonist."],"k":"Chemo nausea = adverse.","rn":[["","Chemotherapy nausea and vomiting is an example of what?","Chemotherapy nausea and vomiting is an example of what?","Adverse (harmful) side effect"]]},{"id":30006,"t":"Pharmacodynamics and Drug Effects","s":"A sedating antidepressant is moved from morning to night dosing. What principle does this illustrate?","o":["Context decides whether a side effect harms or helps","It shows sleepiness is the drug’s therapeutic effect","It shows toxicity falls when doses are given at night","It shows antidepressants are inverse agonists","It shows tolerance develops overnight"],"e":"Some older antidepressants induce sleep. In the morning that can be harmful (e.g. driving); at night, better sleep can help mood, so the side effect becomes an advantage. “Think always about the quality of life of the patient.”","w":["The therapeutic effect is on mood.","The dose is the same.","Not discussed.","Not discussed."],"k":"Same side effect, different context.","rn":[["","What decides if a side effect is harmful?","What decides if a side effect is harmful?","The patient's quality of life"]]},{"id":30007,"t":"Summary","s":"Which statement is FALSE?","o":["An adverse effect is a group of harmful effects that include side and toxic effects","The EC50 is the concentration of drug that produces a half-maximal response","A competitive antagonist shifts the agonist curve right without changing the maximum","Changes in receptor numbers can cause tolerance to drug effects","A partial agonist cannot reach a full agonist’s maximum even at its highest dose"],"e":"The side effect is the umbrella that includes adverse effects; toxic effects come from a higher dose. So the first statement is false. The others are true: EC50 definition, competitive reversible antagonism, receptor changes causing tolerance, and the partial agonist definition.","w":["True – EC50 definition.","True – surmountable antagonism.","True – up/down-regulation.","True – partial agonist."],"k":"Side effect is the umbrella, not adverse.","rn":[["","Is this true: an adverse effect includes side and toxic effects?","Is this true: an adverse effect includes side and toxic effects?","False; Side effects are the umbrella term"],["","Are all side effects harmful?","Are all side effects harmful?","No; Adverse effects are the harmful ones"]]},{"id":30008,"t":"Pharmacodynamics and Drug Effects","s":"Which two mechanisms produce side effects?","o":["Same receptor elsewhere, or other receptor types","Same receptor at one site only, or a higher dose than normal","Binding to DNA; or blocking all ion channels","Placebo response; or chemical antagonism","Enzyme induction; or renal excretion"],"e":"Side effects occur when the drug interacts with the same type of receptor at different sites around the body, or with more than one type of receptor. Most can be predicted by knowing the pharmacology of the drug – a reason to study clinical pharmacology.","w":["A higher dose gives toxic effects.","Not the slide mechanisms.","Not.","Those are PK topics."],"k":"Side effects: same receptor elsewhere or other receptors.","rn":[["","What are the 2 ways side effects occur?","What are the 2 ways side effects occur?","Same receptor type at different body sites; More than one type of receptor"]]},{"id":30009,"t":"Pharmacodynamics and Drug Effects","s":"Why does warning patients about predictable side effects matter?","o":["A warned patient is less likely to stop the drug","It allows every side effect to be completely prevented in advance","It means no drug needs to be monitored","It means patients never need to be told about effects","It means placebo can replace the drug"],"e":"Because side effects are predictable, you can tell the patient what will happen and what they can do to feel better. If you just send them home and they get the adverse effect, they will stop the medication “and then it’s all downhill from there.” No drug is free of side effects.","w":["No drug is free of side effects.","Not stated.","The point is to tell them.","Not."],"k":"Predict → support → adherence."},{"id":30010,"t":"Individual Drug Response","s":"Three people take codeine: one is fine, one develops respiratory depression and one gets no pain relief. What explains this?","o":["A small genetic difference in an enzyme","Different brand names of codeine","A difference in the drug’s chemical structure","Constitutive activity of opioid receptors","Codeine being a direct physicochemical drug"],"e":"Genetics (her PhD topic): small, non-harmful genetic differences (not mutations) make us react differently. With codeine, a small genetic difference in an enzyme leads to very different outcomes in different people.","w":["Same drug.","Same drug.","Not the explanation.","Codeine binds receptors."],"k":"Codeine variability: enzyme genetics.","rn":[["","Codeine and small genetic differences in an enzyme: 3 possible outcomes?","Codeine and small genetic differences in an enzyme: 3 possible outcomes?","Works fine; Respiratory depression; No pain relief"]]},{"id":30011,"t":"Individual Drug Response","s":"Which of these is NOT listed as a factor causing variability in drug response?","o":["Brand name of the medicine","Age (babies and older people)","Genetics","Body composition","Previous exposure"],"e":"Factors: age, sex, genetics, body composition, previous exposure, disease and interactions – leading to pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic variability. In the recording, sleep, alcohol and mental state were also raised.","w":["Age is listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":"Variability factors."},{"id":30012,"t":"Individual Drug Response","s":"Why does age affect drug response?","o":["Immature enzymes in children; declining function in old age","Age changes the generic name and formulation of the drug given","Older people have no receptors left","Children cannot absorb any drug orally","Age only matters for placebo"],"e":"Babies and young children are not fully mature (even enzymes aren’t mature until a certain age), and in older people function declines, so a drug is not broken down the same way at 6 months or at 80 years.","w":["Not the reason.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Age: immature or declining handling.","rn":[["","Why does age affect drug response?","Why does age affect drug response?","Enzymes not mature in the young; Declining in the old; Drug is broken down differently"]]},{"id":30013,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"If individual factors are not taken into account, what problems were named?","o":["No action, a different effect, or stopping","Only a faster effect, as metabolism is more efficient than expected","Only a higher therapeutic index","Only placebo effects","None – they average out"],"e":"A student answered: no action, a different effect, or the patient stops the medication – “we won’t have the effect we want.”","w":["Not named.","Not.","Not.","They don’t."],"k":"Ignoring variability → failure.","rn":[["","What is functional (physiological) antagonism?","What is functional (physiological) antagonism?","Opposing physiological actions; At different receptors"],["","Opioids: example of a side effect that is not harmful?","Opioids: example of a side effect that is not harmful?","Stopping cough (antitussive)"],["","What are the 2 ways side effects occur?","What are the 2 ways side effects occur?","Same receptor type at different body sites; More than one type of receptor"]]},{"id":30014,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"“A drug will not work unless it is bound.” Which drug group is an exception?","o":["Antacids, which act by a direct physicochemical effect","β-blockers, which act by competitive antagonism","Proton pump inhibitors, which act on an ion pump","Thiazides, which block a co-transporter","Antihistamines, which act as inverse agonists"],"e":"Ehrlich’s “magic bullet” idea is true for most drugs, but not all. Antacids act by a direct physicochemical effect (a chemical reaction lowering acidity) without binding. Bulk-forming laxatives and osmotic diuretics also act without binding.","w":["They bind receptors.","They bind the pump.","They bind the transporter.","They bind receptors."],"k":"Antacids: no binding needed.","rn":[["","What are the 3 examples of a direct physicochemical effect?","What are the 3 examples of a direct physicochemical effect?","Bulk forming laxatives; Antacids; Osmotic diuretics"],["","Which molecular mechanism does not need binding?","Which molecular mechanism does not need binding?","Direct physicochemical effect"]]},{"id":30015,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"Which list gives the four molecular mechanisms of drug action?","o":["Physicochemical, transport, enzymes, 4 receptor types","Absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion of the drug","Agonism, antagonism, inverse agonism, tolerance","Ion channels, GPCRs, kinases, nuclear receptors","Transport systems, enzymes, DNA, ribosomes"],"e":"Molecular mechanisms: (1) direct physicochemical effect; (2) transport systems; (3) enzymes; (4) interaction with the 4 major types of receptors. Others: adhesion molecules, organelles and structural proteins.","w":["That is ADME (pharmacokinetics).","Those are types of ligand action.","Those are the four receptor types, only one mechanism.","Not the slide list."],"k":"4 mechanisms.","rn":[["","What are the 4 molecular mechanisms of drug action?","What are the 4 molecular mechanisms of drug action?","Direct physicochemical effect; Transport systems; Enzymes; The 4 major types of receptors"]]},{"id":30016,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"How do osmotic laxatives and osmotic diuretics act?","o":["They “call for water” without binding","They bind gut receptors that open water channels in the lumen","They inhibit enzymes in the gut wall","They block a co-transporter in the kidney","They act as agonists at muscarinic receptors"],"e":"Some laxatives (osmotic laxatives) and osmotic diuretics just “call for water”: they don’t bind to anything and are not even absorbed – a physicochemical effect.","w":["They bind nothing and are not absorbed.","They don’t act on enzymes.","That is thiazides.","Not."],"k":"Osmotic drugs: draw water, no binding.","rn":[["","How do osmotic laxatives and diuretics work?","How do osmotic laxatives and diuretics work?","Draw in water; No binding, not even absorbed"]]},{"id":30017,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"Which pairing of drug and transport target is correct?","o":["Omeprazole – K⁺/H⁺-ATPase in gastric parietal cells","Omeprazole – Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase in cardiac tissue","Digoxin – K⁺/H⁺-ATPase in gastric parietal cells","Thiazides – Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase in the renal tubule","Digoxin – Na⁺/Cl⁻ co-transport in the renal tubule"],"e":"Ion pumps: omeprazole (proton pump inhibitor) – K⁺/H⁺-ATPase in gastric parietal cells; digoxin – Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase in cardiac tissue. Carrier: thiazide diuretics block Na⁺/Cl⁻ co-transport (symport) in the renal tubule.","w":["That is digoxin’s target.","That is omeprazole’s target.","Thiazides block Na⁺/Cl⁻ symport.","Digoxin targets Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase."],"k":"Omeprazole: H⁺/K⁺ pump.","rn":[["","Proton pump inhibitors act on which pump? Where?","Proton pump inhibitors act on which pump? Where?","K+/H+-ATPase; Gastric parietal cells"]]},{"id":30018,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"Thiazide diuretics act on which type of transport system?","o":["A carrier protein (Na⁺/Cl⁻ symport)","An ion pump (Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase)","A ligand-gated ion channel","A G protein-coupled receptor","A direct physicochemical effect"],"e":"Thiazide diuretics block Na⁺/Cl⁻ co-transport in the renal tubule – a transporter (carrier) protein, symport type. Diuretics will be covered fully in the third term.","w":["That is digoxin’s target.","Not.","Not.","Osmotic diuretics act physicochemically."],"k":"Thiazides: Na⁺/Cl⁻ symport.","rn":[["","What are the 2 types of transporter (carrier) proteins?","What are the 2 types of transporter (carrier) proteins?","Symport; Antiport"]]},{"id":30019,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"Ion pumps targeted by drugs move ions in which way?","o":["Against their gradients (active transport)","Down their concentration gradients, without using any energy","Only when a ligand binds an external site","Only when voltage changes","By calling water into the lumen"],"e":"Cell membrane ion pumps carry out active transport of ions against their concentration gradients (see Fundamentals of Physiology). Examples: proton pump (omeprazole), Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase (digoxin).","w":["Pumps use energy to move ions against gradients.","Ligand-gated channels.","Voltage-gated channels.","Osmotic drugs."],"k":"Pumps: active, against gradient.","rn":[["","What do cell membrane ion pumps do?","What do cell membrane ion pumps do?","Active transport of ions; Against their concentration gradients"]]},{"id":30020,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"How can drugs modulate voltage-gated ion channels, which are not proper receptors?","o":["Directly, or via GPCR activation","Only by binding their ligand site, as with nicotinic receptors","Only through nuclear receptors","Only through chemical antagonism","They cannot be modulated by drugs"],"e":"Voltage-gated channels are not proper receptors, but drugs can modulate them directly or as a consequence of activating GPCRs (the G protein can also interact with channels, e.g. potassium channels).","w":["Voltage-gated channels have no ligand site.","Not.","Not.","They can."],"k":"Voltage-gated: direct or via GPCR.","rn":[["","What are the 2 ways a drug can modulate ion channels?","What are the 2 ways a drug can modulate ion channels?","Directly (voltage- / ligand-gated); Second-messenger-regulated via a GPCR"]]},{"id":30021,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"Which are the two ways a drug can inhibit an enzyme?","o":["Bind the active site, or a site changing shape","Bind the substrate itself, or increase the enzyme’s transcription","Bind the product, or lower the temperature","Bind DNA, or block ribosomes","Bind albumin, or speed excretion"],"e":"A drug can inhibit an enzyme by binding the active site (the enzyme cannot bind its substrate), or binding another site that changes its conformation (again the substrate cannot bind). Drugs might also activate the enzyme or increase its transcription.","w":["Increasing transcription increases the enzyme.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Enzyme inhibition: active or other site.","rn":[["","What are the 2 ways a drug can inhibit an enzyme?","What are the 2 ways a drug can inhibit an enzyme?","Bind the active site; Bind another site and change its conformation"]]},{"id":30022,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"Aspirin, ibuprofen and paracetamol all inhibit which enzyme?","e":"They inhibit cyclooxygenase (COX); aspirin does so irreversibly, so platelets (which can't make more enzyme) are affected for their lifespan.","k":"Cyclooxygenase.","a":["cyclooxygenase","cox","cyclo-oxygenase","cox enzyme","prostaglandin synthase"]},{"id":30023,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"A first-line drug class for Alzheimer's disease inhibits which enzyme?","e":"Acetylcholinesterase inhibitors raise ACh levels.","k":"Acetylcholinesterase.","a":["acetylcholinesterase","ache","cholinesterase"],"rn":[["","Acetylcholinesterase: drug class and example?","Acetylcholinesterase: drug class and example?","AChE inhibitors; Neostigmine"]]},{"id":30024,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"Do most drugs acting on enzymes inhibit or potentiate them?","o":["Inhibit","Potentiate","Neither – they only bind substrates","Equal numbers of each","Only increase transcription"],"e":"Most drugs acting on enzymes inhibit rather than potentiate them. Increasing an enzyme’s transcription is what happens in enzyme induction in drug metabolism.","w":["Most inhibit.","Not.","Not stated.","That is induction."],"k":"Mostly inhibit.","rn":[["","Besides inhibiting, what 2 other things can drugs do to enzymes?","Besides inhibiting, what 2 other things can drugs do to enzymes?","Activate the enzyme; Increase its genetic transcription"]]},{"id":30025,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","f":"md15","s":"Which pairing of receptor type, speed and example is correct?","o":["Kinase-linked – hours – cytokine receptors","Ligand-gated ion channel – seconds – muscarinic receptor","GPCR – milliseconds – nicotinic receptor","Nuclear – seconds – oestrogen receptor","Kinase-linked – milliseconds – oestrogen receptor"],"e":"Ligand-gated ion channels: milliseconds (nicotinic ACh). GPCRs: seconds (muscarinic ACh). Kinase-linked: hours (cytokine receptors). Nuclear: hours (oestrogen receptor).","w":["Ion channels: ms; muscarinic is GPCR.","GPCR: seconds; nicotinic is an ion channel.","Nuclear: hours.","Kinase-linked: hours; oestrogen is nuclear."],"k":"Receptor speeds.","rn":[["","How fast are kinase-linked receptor responses? Example?","How fast are kinase-linked receptor responses? Example?","Hours; Cytokine receptors"]]},{"id":30026,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","s":"Are ligand-gated ion channels found only in the nervous system?","o":["No – also heart and muscle","Yes – only neurons depolarise, so muscle uses voltage-gated channels","Yes – muscle uses only GPCRs","No – they exist only in the kidney","No – only in the gut"],"e":"They are also in the heart and muscles. “Depolarisation is not a brain-tissue-unique creature”: it happens in smooth muscle, cardiac muscle and brain, with different machinery.","w":["“Depolarisation is not a brain-tissue-unique creature.”","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Depolarisation in many tissues.","rn":[["","Salbutamol activates which G protein? Result?","Salbutamol activates which G protein? Result?","Gαs; Smooth muscle relaxes (bronchodilation)"]]},{"id":30027,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","s":"What gives a ligand-gated ion channel its ion selectivity?","o":["Its subunits, which attract or repel ions","Its G protein, which chooses which ion is allowed through","Its kinase domain","The drug that binds it","The second messenger it makes"],"e":"Ion selectivity comes from the subunits (“fingers”): they attract or repel certain ions, so a sodium channel would not allow chloride.","w":["Ion channels have no G protein.","Not.","The ligand opens it.","No second messenger."],"k":"Subunits set selectivity.","rn":[["","What decides which ions a ligand-gated channel lets through?","What decides which ions a ligand-gated channel lets through?","Its subunits; Attract or repel certain ions"]]},{"id":30028,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","s":"How does a kinase-linked receptor signal after the ligand binds?","o":["It dimerises; its enzyme phosphorylates","It opens a pore so ions flow through it within milliseconds","It swaps GDP for GTP on its α subunit","It moves into the nucleus to bind DNA","It is degraded, releasing a second messenger"],"e":"Kinase-linked receptors are transmembrane with an enzyme (usually a tyrosine kinase) as part of them. The ligand binds, the receptor usually forms a dimer, its enzyme is activated, and it phosphorylates.","w":["That is a ligand-gated ion channel.","GPCR.","Nuclear receptor.","Not."],"k":"Kinase-linked: dimerise → phosphorylate.","rn":[["","Kinase-linked receptor: what happens when the ligand binds?","Kinase-linked receptor: what happens when the ligand binds?","Receptor forms a dimer; Enzyme is activated; Phosphorylates targets"]]},{"id":30029,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","s":"Why do nuclear/cytoplasmic receptors take a while to act?","o":["They act by changing gene transcription","They must first open an ion pore","They must first activate a G protein","They are found only in the liver","They need a second drug to activate them"],"e":"The molecules go directly through the membrane to receptors in the cytoplasm or nucleus; they take a while because they influence genetic transcription (hours).","w":["Ion channels.","GPCRs.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Nuclear: slow, transcription.","rn":[["","What steps follow a nuclear receptor being activated?","What steps follow a nuclear receptor being activated?","Gene transcription; Protein synthesis; Cellular effects"],["","What steps follow a kinase-linked receptor being activated?","What steps follow a kinase-linked receptor being activated?","Protein phosphorylation; Gene transcription; Protein synthesis; Cellular effects"]]},{"id":30030,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","f":"md16","s":"In the G protein cycle, what happens when an agonist occupies the receptor?","o":["GTP replaces GDP on α, which activates its target","GDP replaces GTP on α, which activates its target","The receptor moves to the target enzyme","βγ binds GTP and activates adenylate cyclase","The receptor opens an ion pore"],"e":"At rest α has GDP bound. The agonist causes a conformational change, and GTP replaces GDP on α (“a boom of energy”). α activates the first enzyme (target 1); βγ can activate target 2. α’s autocatalytic activity hydrolyses GTP to GDP, returning to rest.","w":["Reverse.","The receptor doesn’t move; α does.","α carries GTP.","GPCRs form no pore."],"k":"Agonist → GTP for GDP on α.","rn":[["","What activates the G protein?","What activates the G protein?","An agonist binds the receptor; GTP replaces GDP"]]},{"id":30031,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","s":"Which component actually moves during GPCR signalling?","o":["The α subunit","The receptor itself","The ligand into the cell","The whole βγ complex into the nucleus","The second messenger to the membrane"],"e":"“The receptor itself doesn’t move; it is the α subunit that moves.” The receptor is the transmembrane part (7 passes) plus the G protein.","w":["It doesn’t move.","The ligand stays outside.","Not.","Not."],"k":"α moves.","rn":[["","Which subunit causes the major effect?","Which subunit causes the major effect?","Alpha subunit"]]},{"id":30032,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","s":"Which is the best definition of a second messenger?","o":["The first molecule increased after activation","The enzyme activated by the α subunit, e.g. adenylate cyclase","The ligand that binds the receptor","The βγ subunit after it dissociates","The protein kinase at the end of the pathway"],"e":"“The second messenger is whatever molecule is first increased in concentration within the cell as a reaction of the receptor being activated” – the product of the enzyme reaction, e.g. Gs → adenylate cyclase → cAMP. The second messenger is not the enzyme.","w":["The enzyme makes the second messenger.","That is the first messenger.","Not.","Downstream."],"k":"2nd messenger = first molecule increased.","rn":[["","What is a second messenger?","What is a second messenger?","Molecule first increased in concentration in the cell; After the receptor is activated; Product of the enzyme reaction"]]},{"id":30033,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","s":"Which statement about βγ is correct?","o":["Same activation step; e.g. K⁺ channels in heart","It needs a separate ligand to be activated after the α subunit leaves","It is the main target of salbutamol","It makes cAMP from ATP","It is the receptor’s ligand-binding site"],"e":"βγ needs no separate action – it is part of the same activation. We know much less about it; with some receptors in the heart it activates potassium channels.","w":["It is part of the same activation.","Salbutamol works through Gαs.","Adenylate cyclase.","Not."],"k":"βγ: K⁺ channels in heart."},{"id":30034,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","s":"Roughly what proportion of the drugs we take act on GPCRs?","o":["Around half","Around 5%","Around 90%","Around 10%","None"],"e":"Around half the drugs we take activate or inhibit GPCRs. Parasympathetic and sympathetic actions through muscarinic and adrenergic receptors all work through GPCRs.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"~Half of drugs: GPCRs.","rn":[["","How many drugs act on GPCRs?","How many drugs act on GPCRs?","About half"]]},{"id":30035,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","s":"Which row for Gαq is correct?","o":["Phospholipase C → IP3 (Ca²⁺) and DAG (PKC)","Adenylate cyclase activated → cAMP ↑ → PKA","Adenylate cyclase inhibited → cAMP ↓","Phospholipase C → cAMP → PKA","Guanylate cyclase → cGMP → PKC"],"e":"Gαs: adenylate cyclase activated, cAMP ↑, PKA. Gαq: phospholipase C, IP3 (↑ Ca²⁺ from stores) and DAG (PKC). Gαi: adenylate cyclase inhibited, cAMP ↓.","w":["Gαs.","Gαi.","PLC makes IP3/DAG.","Not."],"k":"Gαq: PLC → IP3/DAG.","rn":[["","Gq: enzyme, second messenger, next step?","Gq: enzyme, second messenger, next step?","Phospholipase C; IP3; Ca2+ and protein kinase C"]]},{"id":30036,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","s":"Gαi is described as “activated but inhibitory”. What does this mean?","o":["The agonist activates Gαi, which then inhibits adenylate cyclase","An antagonist blocks Gαi, which inhibits adenylate cyclase","Gαi inhibits the receptor so no agonist can bind","Gαi is activated by GDP rather than GTP","Gαi inhibits phospholipase C"],"e":"Gαi: we activate it, but it has an inhibitory action – adenylate cyclase is inhibited, so cAMP falls and there is less of the rest of the pathway.","w":["An agonist activates it.","Not.","GTP.","It targets adenylate cyclase."],"k":"Gαi: activated, inhibits AC.","rn":[["","Gi: enzyme and effect?","Gi: enzyme and effect?","Inhibits adenylyl cyclase; cAMP falls"],["","Gs: enzyme, second messenger, next step?","Gs: enzyme, second messenger, next step?","Activates adenylyl cyclase; cAMP; Protein kinase A"]]},{"id":30037,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","s":"Salbutamol relaxes smooth muscle by activating which G protein?","o":["Gαs","Gαq","Gαi","βγ only","None – it is an ion channel agonist"],"e":"Salbutamol activates Gαs and relaxes smooth muscle. It binds the same receptor as noradrenaline/adrenaline and is β2-selective.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","GPCR."],"k":"Salbutamol: Gαs.","rn":[["","Salbutamol activates which G protein? Result?","Salbutamol activates which G protein? Result?","Gαs; Smooth muscle relaxes (bronchodilation)"]]},{"id":30038,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"The end result of a G protein pathway (contraction vs relaxation) depends on what?","o":["The cell and tissue it is in","Only the G protein type, whatever tissue it is found in","Only the agonist’s brand","Only the second messenger","Only the receptor’s 7 passes"],"e":"What the pathway ends in (smooth muscle contraction or relaxation, cardiac contraction, etc.) depends on the cell and tissue; the first part is the same wherever the G protein is.","w":["The first part is the same; the end depends on the tissue.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"End effect depends on tissue.","rn":[["","Digoxin acts on which pump? Where?","Digoxin acts on which pump? Where?","Na+/K+-ATPase; Cardiac tissue"]]},{"id":30039,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Which is NOT part of the definition of a receptor?","o":["A molecule that creates new physiological functions","A protein molecule on the surface or inside the cell","A protein recognition molecule for a chemical mediator","Receives an external signal and produces a cellular response","Mostly on the surface, but can be inside the cell"],"e":"A receptor is a protein molecule on the surface/inside the cell, a protein recognition molecule for a chemical mediator, that receives an external signal and produces a cellular response. Drugs modulate intrinsic functions; they don’t create new ones.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed in recording."],"k":"Receptor definition.","rn":[["","Do drugs \"have\" effects?","Do drugs \"have\" effects?","No; They modulate intrinsic physiological functions"]]},{"id":30040,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"“A drug modulates intrinsic physiological functions.” Which statement captures this?","o":["It changes the amplitude and speed of existing functions","It creates new functions that were not there before","It removes receptors from all cells","It only works by physicochemical effects","It produces effects without binding"],"e":"Drug-receptor interaction: a drug can change the amplitude and speed of body functions. In the cake analogy, the cake didn’t create digestion – drugs just increase or decrease what is already there.","w":["Drugs create nothing.","Not.","Most act via receptors.","Most bind."],"k":"Modulate amplitude and speed.","rn":[["","How does a drug change body functions?","How does a drug change body functions?","Changes their amplitude and speed"]]},{"id":30041,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"A drug produces the maximal response while occupying only some of its receptors. What type of drug is it?","o":["Full agonist","Partial agonist – it never reaches the maximum signal","Antagonist – blocks the space","Inverse agonist – reduces baseline activity","Chemical antagonist – inactivates the agonist"],"e":"Favourite cake = full agonist: full signal, and it doesn’t need to occupy all receptors. “It’ll do” cake = partial agonist: cannot reach the maximum even binding all receptors. Hated cake in the mouth = antagonist: occupies the space but no signalling.","w":["That is the “it’ll do” cake.","That is the hated cake.","Not in the cake game.","Not."],"k":"Favourite cake = full agonist.","rn":[["","Does a full agonist need to occupy all receptors for full response?","Does a full agonist need to occupy all receptors for full response?","No"],["","Cake analogy: full vs partial agonist?","Cake analogy: full vs partial agonist?","Full: favourite cake, full happiness; Partial: \"stress\" cake, never full happiness"],["","What are the 2 types of agonist?","What are the 2 types of agonist?","Full agonist; Partial agonist"]]},{"id":30042,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Name the receptor site where an agonist binds, the same as the endogenous ligand.","e":"Agonists and competitive antagonists bind the orthosteric site; allosteric antagonists bind elsewhere.","k":"Orthosteric site.","a":["orthosteric site","orthosteric","active site","orthosteric binding site"],"rn":[["","Where does an agonist bind?","Where does an agonist bind?","Orthosteric binding site; Same site as the natural ligand"]]},{"id":30043,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Which pairing of drug and agonist type is correct?","o":["Morphine – full agonist; buprenorphine – partial agonist","Morphine – partial agonist; buprenorphine – full agonist","Naloxone – full agonist; morphine – antagonist","Buprenorphine – inverse agonist","Atropine – full agonist"],"e":"Full agonist: morphine (100% signal). Partial agonist: buprenorphine (<100% signal). Naloxone and atropine are competitive antagonists.","w":["Swapped.","Naloxone is an antagonist.","Partial agonist.","Antagonist."],"k":"Morphine full; buprenorphine partial.","rn":[["","Buprenorphine is an example of what?","Buprenorphine is an example of what?","Partial agonist"],["","Morphine is an example of what?","Morphine is an example of what?","Full agonist"],["","What are the 2 types of agonist?","What are the 2 types of agonist?","Full agonist; Partial agonist"]]},{"id":30044,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"How does a corticosteroid act as an agonist?","o":["It binds cortisol’s site on the GR","It binds an allosteric site on the cortisol receptor, away from cortisol","It creates a new receptor for cortisol","It binds the opioid receptor","It acts without binding"],"e":"A corticosteroid binds the same place as cortisol (endogenous) on the glucocorticoid receptor; morphine binds the opioid receptor where endogenous peptides bind; salbutamol binds the same receptor as noradrenaline/adrenaline. “We have a ligand for everything.”","w":["Agonists bind the orthosteric site.","Drugs create nothing.","Morphine.","It binds."],"k":"Corticosteroid = cortisol site.","rn":[["","Where does an agonist bind?","Where does an agonist bind?","Orthosteric binding site; Same site as the natural ligand"],["","What is a competitive reversible antagonist?","What is a competitive reversible antagonist?","Binds the same site as the agonist; Binds and unbinds; Surmountable"],["","What is an irreversible antagonist?","What is an irreversible antagonist?","Binds irreversibly; Same site as the agonist; Unsurmountable"]]},{"id":30045,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Which statement about competitive reversible antagonists is correct?","o":["Same site; surmountable; curve shifts right, same maximum","Different site; unsurmountable; maximum response falls","Same site; unsurmountable; maximum response falls","Different site; surmountable; curve shifts left","No binding; inactivates the agonist"],"e":"Competitive reversible: binds the same (orthosteric) site and can unbind; surmountable by adding agonist; shifts the agonist curve right with the same maximum. Examples: β-blockers, atropine, naloxone, prazosin. Irreversible and allosteric antagonists lower the maximum.","w":["Allosteric.","Irreversible.","Not.","Chemical antagonism."],"k":"Competitive reversible: right shift, same max.","rn":[["","Image card: Agonist curves with rising antagonist concentration. What type of antagonist is this? H…","Agonist curves with rising antagonist concentration. What type of antagonist is this? How can you tell?","Competitive reversible; Curve shifts right; Maximal response unchanged (surmountable)"],["","Competitive reversible antagonist: effect on the agonist curve?","Competitive reversible antagonist: effect on the agonist curve?","Shifts right; Same maximal response"]]},{"id":30046,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Why can adding more agonist overcome a competitive reversible antagonist?","o":["Adding more agonist can win back the receptors","The antagonist permanently removes chairs","The agonist and antagonist share each chair","More chairs appear when the antagonist is added","The music stops when the antagonist binds"],"e":"There are only so many receptors (chairs); add more agonist and it can still reach 100% – “if you have 50 against 10, the 50 have a better chance to win.” So the curve shifts right (potency lost) but the same effect can be reached.","w":["Irreversible.","Not.","Not here.","Not."],"k":"Musical chairs: surmountable.","rn":[["","What is a partial agonist?","What is a partial agonist?","Produces less than 100% response; Even when bound to all receptors"],["","Which drugs need affinity?","Which drugs need affinity?","All receptor-acting drugs; Agonists and antagonists"],["","What is chemical antagonism?","What is chemical antagonism?","Binds to and inactivates the agonist; Not the receptor"]]},{"id":30047,"t":"Selectivity, Desensitisation and Tolerance","s":"Phenoxybenzamine binds the orthosteric site and does not unbind. What happens to the agonist curve?","o":["The maximum response falls","It shifts right with the same maximum","It shifts left","It is unchanged","It rises above 100%"],"e":"Irreversible antagonists bind and don’t unbind; you only get a response from unoccupied receptors, so the maximum response falls (unsurmountable). After the lecture she added that the receptor “needs to be produced again”.","w":["Reversible competitive.","Not.","It changes.","Not."],"k":"Irreversible: max falls.","rn":[["","Long-term agonist: what does the body do?","Long-term agonist: what does the body do?","Removes receptors; Response falls"],["","EC50: the concentration that does what?","EC50: the concentration that does what?","Response halfway between baseline and maximum; After a specified exposure time"]]},{"id":30048,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"A noncompetitive (allosteric) antagonist acts how?","o":["Binds another site, so the agonist can’t bind","Binds the agonist’s site and unbinds easily, so more agonist overcomes it","Binds and inactivates the agonist in solution","Has opposing actions at a different receptor","Binds the resting state, lowering baseline activity"],"e":"Noncompetitive (true definition): binds a non-orthosteric (allosteric) site and changes the conformation, so the agonist can no longer bind; no competition, unsurmountable, maximum response drops.","w":["That is competitive reversible.","Chemical.","Functional.","Inverse agonist."],"k":"Allosteric: different site, unsurmountable.","rn":[["","What is a noncompetitive (allosteric) antagonist?","What is a noncompetitive (allosteric) antagonist?","Binds a different (allosteric) site; Changes receptor conformation; Unsurmountable"],["","What are the 2 ways a drug can inhibit an enzyme?","What are the 2 ways a drug can inhibit an enzyme?","Bind the active site; Bind another site and change its conformation"],["","What is an irreversible antagonist?","What is an irreversible antagonist?","Binds irreversibly; Same site as the agonist; Unsurmountable"]]},{"id":30049,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Why is aspirin’s irreversible effect on platelets significant?","o":["Platelets can’t make more enzyme","Platelets make enzyme faster than other cells, so higher doses are needed","Aspirin is reversible in platelets only","Platelets lack cyclooxygenase","It means higher doses are needed"],"e":"Aspirin is irreversible on platelets: our cells can make more enzyme, but platelets cannot – which is why aspirin is given at a much lower dose as an anti-platelet. (Exact doses were not fully clear on the recording – “don’t memorise”.)","w":["Platelets cannot replace it, so low doses suffice.","Irreversible.","They have it.","Lower dose."],"k":"Aspirin: irreversible in platelets."},{"id":30050,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Adrenaline raises arterial pressure and histamine lowers it, acting on different receptors. Name this type of antagonism.","e":"Physiological antagonism: opposing effects through different receptors.","k":"Physiological antagonism.","a":["physiological antagonism","physiological","physiologic antagonism","functional antagonism"],"rn":[["","What is functional (physiological) antagonism?","What is functional (physiological) antagonism?","Opposing physiological actions; At different receptors"]]},{"id":30051,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Dimercaprol binds arsenic or mercury and inactivates it. Name this type of antagonism.","e":"Chemical antagonism: the antagonist binds and inactivates the agonist/toxin; physiological antagonism uses opposing receptors.","k":"Chemical antagonism.","a":["chemical antagonism","chemical","chemical antagonist"],"rn":[["","What is chemical antagonism?","What is chemical antagonism?","Binds to and inactivates the agonist; Not the receptor"]]},{"id":30052,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Which receptors are examples of constitutively active receptors?","o":["Histamine and some GABA receptors","Nicotinic and muscarinic acetylcholine receptors in the heart","β1 and β2 adrenoceptors","Oestrogen and cytokine receptors","Opioid and glucocorticoid receptors"],"e":"Some receptors have background activity without an agonist – constitutively active receptors, e.g. histamine receptors and some GABA receptors. The two-state model says receptors can be active or inactive.","w":["Not the examples given.","Not listed.","Not listed.","Not listed."],"k":"Constitutive: histamine, GABA.","rn":[["","What does an antihistamine (inverse agonist) do at the histamine H1-receptor?","What does an antihistamine (inverse agonist) do at the histamine H1-receptor?","Keeps the receptor inactive; Stops itching, swelling, inflammation"]]},{"id":30053,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","f":"md25","s":"In the two-state model, which pairing is correct?","o":["Inverse agonist favours R; antagonist binds both","Agonist favours R; inverse agonist favours R*; antagonist binds R only","Antagonist favours R*; agonist binds both","Inverse agonist favours R*; antagonist favours R","All three favour R*"],"e":"The agonist favours R* (active); the inverse agonist favours R (resting); the antagonist binds both and causes no shift.","w":["Agonist favours R*.","Not.","Reversed.","Not."],"k":"Agonist R*, inverse R, antagonist both.","rn":[["","Two-state model: which state does each ligand bind?","Two-state model: which state does each ligand bind?","Agonist: active state; Inverse agonist: resting state; Neutral antagonist: both"]]},{"id":30054,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Pimavanserin holds 5-HT2A receptors in the inactive state, reducing constitutive activity. What type of drug is it?","e":"Inverse agonists favour the inactive receptor state; most “antihistamines” are H1 inverse agonists.","k":"Inverse agonist.","a":["inverse agonist","inverse agonists"],"rn":[["","What is Inverse agonist?","What is Inverse agonist?","Binds active receptors; Moves them to the inactive state; Negative activity"],["","Pimavanserin is an inverse agonist of which receptor? Use?","Pimavanserin is an inverse agonist of which receptor? Use?","5-HT2A; Antipsychotic"],["","Most antihistamines are actually what?","Most antihistamines are actually what?","Inverse agonists"]]},{"id":30055,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Most “antihistamines” are actually which type of drug?","o":["Inverse agonists that hold H1 receptors inactive","Neutral antagonists that bind both states equally","Partial agonists at H1 receptors","Chemical antagonists that inactivate histamine","Full agonists at H1 receptors"],"e":"More drugs we call antagonists turn out to be inverse agonists. Most antihistamines bind the resting state and keep the receptor there; the end effect looks like blocking, so they were confused with antagonists.","w":["Most are inverse agonists.","Not.","They don’t bind histamine.","Not."],"k":"Antihistamines: inverse agonists.","rn":[["","What does an antihistamine (inverse agonist) do at the histamine H1-receptor?","What does an antihistamine (inverse agonist) do at the histamine H1-receptor?","Keeps the receptor inactive; Stops itching, swelling, inflammation"],["","What is Inverse agonist?","What is Inverse agonist?","Binds active receptors; Moves them to the inactive state; Negative activity"],["","Most antihistamines are actually what?","Most antihistamines are actually what?","Inverse agonists"]]},{"id":30056,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Why does a GABA antagonist cause convulsions?","o":["GABA receptors have constitutive activity, which is lost","GABA antagonists are full agonists at glutamate receptors","GABA antagonists inhibit acetylcholinesterase","GABA antagonists block sodium pumps","GABA receptors have no baseline activity"],"e":"GABA relates to relaxation and reduced anxiety. Giving a GABA antagonist causes convulsions because these receptors have constitutive activity, so antagonising them has a consequence.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","They do."],"k":"GABA constitutive activity.","rn":[["","Two-state model: what 2 states can receptors be in?","Two-state model: what 2 states can receptors be in?","Active (constitutive activity); Inactive (resting)"]]},{"id":30057,"t":"Selectivity, Desensitisation and Tolerance","s":"Name the β-blocker that binds β1 and β2 adrenoceptors equally well.","e":"Propranolol is non-selective; metoprolol is relatively β1-selective, with less effect on the lungs.","k":"Propranolol.","a":["propranolol"],"rn":[["","Propranolol binds which adrenoceptors?","Propranolol binds which adrenoceptors?","β1 and β2 equally"]]},{"id":30058,"t":"Selectivity, Desensitisation and Tolerance","s":"Why is no drug completely selective?","o":["At higher doses it binds other receptors","All receptors are identical in structure, so no drug can tell them apart","Drugs create new receptors at higher doses","Selectivity only applies to antagonists","Selectivity falls at lower doses"],"e":"No drug is completely selective; selectivity depends on dose. Once a β2-preferring drug occupies β2 receptors, it binds β1 (“you’re close enough”) and more adverse effects start – selective until a certain dose.","w":["Selectivity is lost at higher doses.","Not.","All drugs.","Higher doses."],"k":"Selectivity is dose-dependent.","rn":[["","Why does selectivity fail at higher doses?","Why does selectivity fail at higher doses?","Preferred receptors fill up; Drug binds similar receptors; More adverse effects"]]},{"id":30059,"t":"Selectivity, Desensitisation and Tolerance","s":"Which pairing of term and time course is correct?","o":["Tachyphylaxis minutes; tolerance hours–weeks","Desensitisation over weeks; tolerance within minutes of the first dose","Both – minutes","Both – years","Tolerance – seconds; tachyphylaxis – days"],"e":"Desensitisation or tachyphylaxis: the effect diminishes with continuous or repeated dosing over minutes. Tolerance: a more gradual decrease over hours/days/weeks (e.g. opiates, benzodiazepines, barbiturates, stimulants).","w":["Swapped.","Different.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Tachyphylaxis minutes; tolerance hours–weeks.","rn":[["","What is tolerance?","What is tolerance?","More gradual decrease in responsiveness; Over hours / days / weeks"],["","What is desensitisation (tachyphylaxis)?","What is desensitisation (tachyphylaxis)?","Effect gradually diminishes with continuous or repeated dosing; Over minutes"]]},{"id":30060,"t":"Selectivity, Desensitisation and Tolerance","s":"A patient on a β-blocker for months develops more β-adrenoceptors. What is the risk if the drug is stopped suddenly?","o":["More receptors for the agonist: tolerance","Fewer receptors, so a stronger response to the same blocker dose","No change, as receptor numbers are fixed","Immediate toxicity","Conversion of the blocker into an agonist"],"e":"Receptor numbers are not fixed. With a blocker, the body may make more receptors, so there are more for the agonist while the antagonist dose stays the same – tolerance. With an agonist, the body may remove receptors (translocation), reducing response. This is common with β-adrenoceptors.","w":["The body makes more receptors.","Not fixed.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Blocker → up-regulation → tolerance.","rn":[["","Long-term β-blocker: what does the body do?","Long-term β-blocker: what does the body do?","Makes more receptors; Tolerance; need higher dose"]]},{"id":30061,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"Which property must every receptor-acting drug have, whether agonist or antagonist?","e":"All need affinity (binding); only agonists have efficacy. High affinity + zero efficacy = antagonist.","k":"Affinity.","a":["affinity"],"rn":[["","Which drugs need affinity?","Which drugs need affinity?","All receptor-acting drugs; Agonists and antagonists"],["","Is this true: an antagonist has zero affinity?","Is this true: an antagonist has zero affinity?","False; Antagonists need affinity"]]},{"id":30062,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"On a 0–10 efficacy scale, which ranking is correct?","o":["Full agonist 10; partial agonist between; antagonist 0","Full agonist 10; antagonist 5; partial agonist 0","Antagonist 10; partial agonist 5; full agonist 0","All 10, as all have affinity","Partial agonist 10; full agonist 5"],"e":"Full agonist 10, antagonist 0 (“an antagonist has no efficacy; we don’t measure it”), and a partial agonist somewhere in between (5, 6, 8 or 2).","w":["Antagonist 0.","Reversed.","Affinity ≠ efficacy.","Not."],"k":"Efficacy scale.","rn":[["","Efficacy on 0-10: full agonist, partial agonist, antagonist?","Efficacy on 0-10: full agonist, partial agonist, antagonist?","Full: 10; Partial: between 0 and 10; Antagonist: 0"]]},{"id":30063,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Which statement is TRUE?","o":["A partial agonist can’t match a full agonist’s maximum","A receptor antagonist is a drug with zero affinity for the receptor it blocks","A curve to the right on a log dose-response plot means higher potency","A full agonist only reaches maximum when all receptors are occupied","An adverse effect is the umbrella term that includes side effects"],"e":"True: a partial agonist cannot achieve the same maximal response (the Victoria sponge). False: antagonists need affinity; a right-shifted curve is less potent; a full agonist doesn’t need all receptors occupied; the side effect is the umbrella.","w":["Antagonists need affinity; they have zero efficacy.","Right = less potent.","It doesn’t need all receptors.","The side effect is the umbrella."],"k":"Partial agonist definition.","rn":[["","What are the 2 types of agonist?","What are the 2 types of agonist?","Full agonist; Partial agonist"],["","Efficacy on 0-10: full agonist, partial agonist, antagonist?","Efficacy on 0-10: full agonist, partial agonist, antagonist?","Full: 10; Partial: between 0 and 10; Antagonist: 0"]]},{"id":30064,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","f":"md32","s":"Four drugs A–D reach the same maximum; A’s curve is furthest left and D’s furthest right. Which statement is correct?","o":["A is the most potent because it has the lowest EC50","D is the most potent because it has the highest EC50","A is the most efficacious because it is furthest left","All are equally potent, as maxima are equal","D has the highest affinity"],"e":"Potency is the amount of drug needed for an effect of given intensity, measured by EC50. The dashed line at 50% crosses A first (lowest EC50), so A is most potent; D (furthest right) is least potent. Equal maxima mean equal efficacy, not equal potency.","w":["Higher EC50 = less potent.","Same maximum = same efficacy.","Potencies differ.","Not shown."],"k":"Left = more potent.","rn":[["","Image card: Which drug is the most potent and which the least? Why?","Which drug is the most potent and which the least? Why?","Most potent: A; Least potent: D; A has the lowest EC50 (furthest left)"]]},{"id":30065,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"“Morphine is more potent than codeine.” What does this mean?","o":["Less morphine gives the same effect","Morphine reaches a higher maximum response than codeine can","Morphine binds more receptor subtypes","Morphine has a longer half-life","Morphine has a wider therapeutic window"],"e":"EC50 is a comparative measure: “When we say morphine is more potent than codeine, it’s because you need less morphine to cause the same effect in 50% of the population.”","w":["That is efficacy, not potency.","That is selectivity.","That is PK.","Not what potency means."],"k":"Potency: less drug needed."},{"id":30066,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"EC50 and ED50 differ how?","o":["EC50: individual half-max; ED50: 50% respond","EC50: dose killing 50% of subjects; ED50: dose toxic in 50% of the population","EC50: in vivo; ED50: in vitro only","They are identical terms","EC50: toxic dose; ED50: lethal dose"],"e":"EC50 is the concentration giving a response halfway between baseline and maximum (half-maximal effect in an individual). In vivo, ED50 is the dose at which 50% of the population obtain the therapeutic effect.","w":["Those are LD50 and TD50.","ED50 is the in vivo term.","They differ.","Not."],"k":"EC50 vs ED50.","rn":[["","EC50 vs ED50: who reaches 50%?","EC50 vs ED50: who reaches 50%?","EC50: 50% of max effect in an individual; ED50: 50% of the population respond"]]},{"id":30067,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","f":"md23","s":"In panel A, increasing antagonist (0, 1, 10, 100, 1000) shifts the agonist curves in parallel to the right, all reaching the same maximum. Which antagonist type is shown?","o":["Reversible competitive","Irreversible competitive","Allosteric noncompetitive","Chemical","Functional"],"e":"(A) Reversible competitive: curves shift right in parallel but reach the same maximum (surmountable). (B) Irreversible competitive: with increasing antagonist the maximum falls.","w":["Maximum falls.","Maximum falls.","Inactivates the agonist.","Opposing receptors."],"k":"Parallel right shift = reversible competitive.","rn":[["","Competitive reversible antagonist: effect on the agonist curve?","Competitive reversible antagonist: effect on the agonist curve?","Shifts right; Same maximal response"],["","Image card: Agonist curves with rising antagonist concentration. What type of antagonist is this? H…","Agonist curves with rising antagonist concentration. What type of antagonist is this? How can you tell?","Competitive reversible; Curve shifts right; Maximal response unchanged (surmountable)"]]},{"id":30068,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"A competitive reversible antagonist is added. What happens to the agonist’s potency and efficacy?","o":["Potency falls; efficacy unchanged","Potency rises; efficacy falls below the original maximum","Both fall","Both rise","Neither changes"],"e":"The curve shifts right: more agonist is needed, so potency is lost, but the same maximum (efficacy) can still be reached – surmountable.","w":["Curve shifts right, same maximum.","Efficacy unchanged.","Not.","Potency changes."],"k":"Competitive: ↓ potency, same efficacy.","rn":[["","What are the 3 parts of drug-receptor interaction?","What are the 3 parts of drug-receptor interaction?","Affinity; Efficacy; Potency"]]},{"id":30069,"t":"Selectivity, Desensitisation and Tolerance","s":"How does the body respond to long-term antagonism?","o":["Changing receptor numbers or natural compound","By destroying the antagonist inside the receptor binding pocket","By converting the antagonist to an agonist","By stopping all receptor production","It cannot respond"],"e":"The body fights antagonists: it can change the number of receptors or produce more of the natural compound.","w":["The body changes receptors or ligand levels.","Not.","Not.","It can."],"k":"Body counters antagonists.","rn":[["","What receptor changes cause tolerance?","What receptor changes cause tolerance?","Conformational changes; Phosphorylation"]]},{"id":30070,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","f":"md30a","s":"In the antagonist classification chart, which category does a physiologic antagonist belong to?","o":["Nonreceptor antagonists","Receptor antagonists at the active site","Receptor antagonists at an allosteric site","Competitive antagonists","Inverse agonists"],"e":"Antagonists divide into receptor antagonists (active site: reversible = competitive; irreversible = noncompetitive active-site; or allosteric site = noncompetitive allosteric) and nonreceptor antagonists (chemical or physiologic).","w":["Not.","Not.","Reversible active-site.","Not in chart."],"k":"Physiologic = nonreceptor.","rn":[["","What are the two kinds of nonreceptor antagonist?","What are the two kinds of nonreceptor antagonist?","Chemical antagonist; Physiological (functional) antagonist"],["","How is antagonist type decided by binding site and reversibility?","How is antagonist type decided by binding site and reversibility?","Active site, reversible: competitive; Active site, irreversible: noncompetitive; Allosteric site: noncompetitive"],["","What are the 3 types of receptor antagonist?","What are the 3 types of receptor antagonist?","Competitive reversible; Irreversible; Noncompetitive (allosteric)"]]},{"id":30071,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Where do irreversible antagonists fit when classifying antagonists?","o":["Books differ; can you compete?","They are always competitive, because they bind the orthosteric site","They are always allosteric","They don’t exist","They are chemical antagonists"],"e":"Irreversibility can apply to both orthosteric and allosteric antagonists. “I really don’t care how you put that classification in your head; it’s different books.” The concept: same site and reversible means you can compete by adding agonist; irreversible or allosteric means you can’t.","w":["Classification varies between books.","Books vary.","They do.","Not."],"k":"Irreversible: classification varies."},{"id":30072,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","f":"md33","s":"Why does a plasma concentration–time curve after an oral dose rise and then fall?","o":["Absorption first, then elimination","Metabolism dominates first, then absorption and distribution take over","The drug is created then destroyed","Receptors fill, then empty","Tolerance develops, then reverses"],"e":"The curve rises while absorption and distribution dominate, peaks (C max at t max), then falls because nothing more is absorbed and elimination and metabolism dominate.","w":["Reversed.","Drugs create nothing.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Rise: absorption; fall: elimination.","rn":[["","Plasma curve: what dominates the rise vs the fall?","Plasma curve: what dominates the rise vs the fall?","Rise: absorption and distribution; Fall: metabolism and elimination"]]},{"id":30073,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"Which pairing of term and meaning is correct?","o":["AUC – overall drug exposure","C max – time to reach peak concentration","t max – highest concentration reached","t½ – time to reach steady state","AUC – maximum response"],"e":"C max: highest concentration after a dose. t max: time to reach C max. t½: time for plasma concentration to halve. AUC: area under the curve = overall drug exposure. R max is maximum response.","w":["That is t max.","That is C max.","Steady state takes 3–5 half-lives.","That is R max."],"k":"AUC = exposure.","rn":[["","What does AUC represent?","What does AUC represent?","Overall drug exposure"]]},{"id":30074,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"About how many half-lives does it take to reach steady-state concentration (give a range)?","e":"Steady state is reached after about 3–5 half-lives.","k":"3–5.","a":["3-5","3 to 5","4-5","4","5","3–5","three to five","4 to 5"],"rn":[["","How many half-lives to reach steady state?","How many half-lives to reach steady state?","About 3 to 5"]]},{"id":30075,"t":"Summary","s":"Why does knowing a drug’s half-life help with dosing intervals?","o":["It shows when to re-dose safely","It sets the drug’s maximum efficacy at the receptor","It defines the drug’s affinity","It decides the drug’s brand","It shows which receptor it binds"],"e":"Half-life is useful to know time to steady state and time for elimination; it tells you when to give the next dose without reaching toxic or sub-therapeutic levels, e.g. why paracetamol can be taken every four, six or eight hours.","w":["Efficacy is R max.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"t½ guides dosing interval.","rn":[["","What is TD50?","What is TD50?","Half maximal toxic dose; Toxic effect in 50% of population"],["","Which two choices give the right drug response?","Which two choices give the right drug response?","Correct choice of drug; Correct dose"],["","What is ED50?","What is ED50?","Half maximal effective dose; 50% of population get therapeutic effect"]]},{"id":30076,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","f":"md34","s":"In the quantal dose-response curves, which ordering is shown?","o":["ED50 < TD50 < LD50","LD50 < TD50 < ED50","TD50 < ED50 < LD50","ED50 = TD50 = LD50","LD50 < ED50 < TD50"],"e":"Cumulative % responding vs dose for therapeutic, toxic and lethal effects; the 50% points give ED50 < TD50 < LD50.","w":["Reversed.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"ED50 < TD50 < LD50.","rn":[["","What is the therapeutic index?","What is the therapeutic index?","TD50 / ED50"]]},{"id":30077,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"The therapeutic index is TD50 divided by which value?","e":"TI = TD50 ÷ ED50; a larger TI is safer.","k":"ED50.","a":["ed50","effective dose 50","median effective dose"],"rn":[["","What is the therapeutic index?","What is the therapeutic index?","TD50 / ED50"]]},{"id":30078,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"Drug X has TD50 = 100 mg and ED50 = 50 mg; drug Y has TD50 = 1000 mg and ED50 = 10 mg. Which statement is correct?","o":["Y has the larger TI and the wider window","X has the larger TI and the wider window","Both have the same TI","X needs less monitoring than Y","Y has a narrow window needing monitoring"],"e":"TI = TD50/ED50: X = 100/50 = 2; Y = 1000/10 = 100. Y has the larger TI (wide window, safer); X has a small TI (narrow window), so it would need therapeutic drug monitoring.","w":["X is 2.","Different.","X needs more.","Y is wide."],"k":"TI calculation."},{"id":30079,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"Which definition of the therapeutic window is correct?","o":["Maximum efficacy, minimum toxicity in most patients","Concentrations at which all patients have side effects but no toxicity","The time between doses","The dose that kills half of subjects","The concentration at half-maximal response"],"e":"Therapeutic window: the range of drug concentrations within which the drug exhibits maximum efficacy (desired effect) and minimum toxicity in the majority of patients. It is quantified by the therapeutic index.","w":["Not the definition.","Dosing interval.","LD50.","EC50."],"k":"Therapeutic window definition.","rn":[["","What is Therapeutic window?","What is Therapeutic window?","Range of drug concentrations; Maximum efficacy, minimum toxicity; In the majority of patients"]]},{"id":30080,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"A drug with a narrow therapeutic window is given with a CYP450 inducer. What risk does this create?","o":["Falling sub-therapeutic","Rising into the toxic range from slower metabolism","No change, as windows are fixed","Conversion to a partial agonist","A wider window"],"e":"A narrow window is dangerous: a simple change (interactions, induction/inhibition of metabolism) can push the level into the toxic or sub-therapeutic range. An inducer speeds metabolism, lowering levels. The lecturer gave warfarin as an example where CP450 induction matters.","w":["An inducer speeds metabolism.","Levels can move.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Narrow window + inducer → sub-therapeutic.","rn":[["","Why are narrow-window drugs dangerous?","Why are narrow-window drugs dangerous?","Small changes push them toxic or sub-therapeutic; e.g. interactions, enzyme induction / inhibition"]]},{"id":30081,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"Is a toxic or a sub-therapeutic level more dangerous?","o":["It depends (e.g. epilepsy)","Always toxic, as poisoning is worse than any lack of effect","Always sub-therapeutic, as nothing happens","Neither is dangerous","Only toxic levels matter"],"e":"The room voted toxic, but “it depends”. In epilepsy, a sub-therapeutic level risks convulsions; with cardiovascular protection drugs, a low level can mean a stroke or heart attack.","w":["Sub-therapeutic levels can be dangerous too.","It depends.","Both can be.","Both matter."],"k":"Both can be dangerous.","rn":[["","When is sub-therapeutic dangerous? 2 examples?","When is sub-therapeutic dangerous? 2 examples?","Epilepsy drugs: convulsions; Cardiovascular protection: stroke, heart attack"]]},{"id":30082,"t":"Pharmacodynamics and Drug Effects","f":"md35","s":"On a plasma concentration–time graph, what does the region above the toxic threshold represent?","o":["Toxic region – adverse effects apparent","Therapeutic window – optimal effect","Sub-therapeutic region – too low","Steady-state region","Placebo region"],"e":"Toxic region (above the toxic threshold): adverse effects apparent. Therapeutic window: optimal, effective without intolerable adverse effects. Sub-therapeutic region: too low for optimal effects. The double dose enters the toxic region.","w":["Between thresholds.","Below.","Not labelled.","Not."],"k":"Above threshold = toxic.","rn":[["","What are the 4 effects of drug action?","What are the 4 effects of drug action?","Therapeutic; Side; Adverse; Toxic; MNEMONIC"],["","Is this true: an adverse effect includes side and toxic effects?","Is this true: an adverse effect includes side and toxic effects?","False; Side effects are the umbrella term"]]},{"id":30083,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"A drug binds the receptor at the same site as the endogenous ligand but produces no response, and extra agonist overcomes it. What is it?","o":["A competitive reversible antagonist","A partial agonist at the same site","An irreversible antagonist","An allosteric antagonist","An inverse agonist"],"e":"Binding the orthosteric site with no effect = antagonist; overcome by more agonist = surmountable = competitive reversible.","w":["It gives no response.","Not surmountable.","Different site.","Reduces baseline."],"k":"Same site + surmountable = competitive.","rn":[["","What is a competitive reversible antagonist?","What is a competitive reversible antagonist?","Binds the same site as the agonist; Binds and unbinds; Surmountable"]]},{"id":30084,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"A drug binds a site away from the agonist site and reduces the maximum agonist response. What is it?","o":["A noncompetitive (allosteric) antagonist","A competitive reversible antagonist","A full agonist","A chemical antagonist","A partial agonist"],"e":"An allosteric antagonist binds a different site, changes conformation so the agonist can’t bind, and is unsurmountable – R max falls.","w":["Same site; max unchanged.","Not.","Binds the agonist.","Same site."],"k":"Allosteric: max falls.","rn":[["","What is a noncompetitive (allosteric) antagonist?","What is a noncompetitive (allosteric) antagonist?","Binds a different (allosteric) site; Changes receptor conformation; Unsurmountable"]]},{"id":30085,"t":"Summary","s":"Buprenorphine (a partial agonist) is given to a patient already receiving morphine. What would you expect?","o":["It gives a smaller maximum response","It gives a larger response than morphine as it binds more receptors","It gives no response at all","It increases morphine’s potency","It inactivates morphine chemically"],"e":"Buprenorphine is a partial agonist: like the “it’ll do” cake, it produces a response but cannot reach the maximum even if it binds all receptors. Occupying receptors, it would give less than morphine’s 100% signal.","w":["Partial agonists have lower maximum.","It gives some response.","Not.","Not chemical."],"k":"Partial agonist: lower maximum.","rn":[["","Which two choices give the right drug response?","Which two choices give the right drug response?","Correct choice of drug; Correct dose"],["","EC50: the concentration that does what?","EC50: the concentration that does what?","Response halfway between baseline and maximum; After a specified exposure time"]]},{"id":30086,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"A patient with opioid overdose is given a drug that competes with the opioid at the same site and can be overcome by more agonist. Name the drug.","e":"Naloxone is a competitive reversible opioid antagonist.","k":"Naloxone.","a":["naloxone"],"rn":[["","Competitive reversible antagonists: 4 examples?","Competitive reversible antagonists: 4 examples?","β-blockers; Atropine; Naloxone; Prazosin"]]},{"id":30087,"t":"Selectivity, Desensitisation and Tolerance","s":"An asthma patient on high-dose salbutamol develops palpitations. Which concept best explains this?","o":["Selectivity is lost at high dose (β1)","Salbutamol is a non-selective β-blocker acting on the heart","Salbutamol is an inverse agonist at β2","Tachyphylaxis increases salbutamol’s effect","Chemical antagonism by salbutamol"],"e":"Salbutamol is β2-selective, but no drug is completely selective: at higher doses, once β2 receptors are occupied, it binds β1 (“close enough”), giving more side effects.","w":["It is a β2-agonist.","Agonist.","Tachyphylaxis reduces effect.","Not."],"k":"High dose → loss of selectivity.","rn":[["","Why does selectivity fail at higher doses?","Why does selectivity fail at higher doses?","Preferred receptors fill up; Drug binds similar receptors; More adverse effects"]]},{"id":30088,"t":"Selectivity, Desensitisation and Tolerance","s":"A patient needs a β-blocker with less effect on the lungs. Name a β1-selective option.","e":"Metoprolol is relatively β1-selective, though selectivity is lost at higher doses.","k":"Metoprolol.","a":["metoprolol","atenolol","bisoprolol"],"rn":[["","Metoprolol binds which adrenoceptors?","Metoprolol binds which adrenoceptors?","β1 selectively (cardioselective)"]]},{"id":30089,"t":"Selectivity, Desensitisation and Tolerance","s":"A patient's response to repeated doses falls within minutes. Name this rapid form of desensitisation.","e":"Tachyphylaxis: minutes; tolerance: hours to weeks.","k":"Tachyphylaxis.","a":["tachyphylaxis","desensitisation","desensitization"],"rn":[["","What is desensitisation (tachyphylaxis)?","What is desensitisation (tachyphylaxis)?","Effect gradually diminishes with continuous or repeated dosing; Over minutes"]]},{"id":30090,"t":"Selectivity, Desensitisation and Tolerance","s":"Which mechanisms of desensitisation/tolerance are correct?","o":["Receptor changes and translocation","Enzyme induction and faster renal excretion of the drug only","Drug creation of new receptors","Chemical antagonism by the body","Placebo response"],"e":"Variety of mechanisms: change in receptors (conformational changes, phosphorylation, NMJ) and translocation of receptors (e.g. β-adrenoceptors: up- and down-regulation).","w":["Receptor changes are listed.","Drugs create nothing.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Receptor change, translocation.","rn":[["","What are the 6 mechanisms of desensitisation and tolerance?","What are the 6 mechanisms of desensitisation and tolerance?","Change in receptors; Translocation of receptors; Exhaustion of mediators; Increased metabolic degradation; Physiological adaptation; Active extrusion of drug from cells"],["","What receptor changes cause tolerance?","What receptor changes cause tolerance?","Conformational changes; Phosphorylation"],["","What is receptor translocation?","What is receptor translocation?","Removing receptors from the membrane"]]},{"id":30091,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"A drug acts on K⁺/H⁺-ATPase in parietal cells. Which mechanism category is this?","o":["Transport system (ion pump)","Direct physicochemical effect","Enzyme (cyclooxygenase)","GPCR","Nuclear receptor"],"e":"Proton pump inhibitors (e.g. omeprazole) act on the K⁺/H⁺-ATPase in gastric parietal cells – a cell membrane ion pump, under transport systems.","w":["Antacids.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"PPIs: ion pump.","rn":[["","What are the 3 transport systems drugs act on?","What are the 3 transport systems drugs act on?","Ion channels; Cell membrane ion pumps; Transporter (carrier) proteins"]]},{"id":30092,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"An antacid and a proton pump inhibitor both reduce stomach acidity. How do their mechanisms differ?","o":["Antacid: chemical reaction; PPI: ion pump","Antacid binds the ion pump; PPI reacts chemically with stomach acid","Both bind GPCRs","Both act by chemical reaction","Both inhibit cyclooxygenase"],"e":"Antacids act by a direct physicochemical effect – a chemical reaction decreasing acidity (raising pH). PPIs (omeprazole) inhibit the K⁺/H⁺-ATPase in parietal cells.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","PPIs bind.","Not."],"k":"Antacid vs PPI.","rn":[["","How do antacids work?","How do antacids work?","Chemical reaction in the stomach; Decrease acidity (increase pH)"]]},{"id":30093,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"Digoxin is described as “quite a potent drug” acting on which target?","o":["Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase in cardiac tissue","K⁺/H⁺-ATPase in parietal cells","Na⁺/Cl⁻ symport in the kidney","Cyclooxygenase","β1 adrenoceptors"],"e":"Digoxin acts on the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase in cardiac tissue (an ion pump).","w":["Omeprazole.","Thiazides.","NSAIDs.","Metoprolol."],"k":"Digoxin: Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","rn":[["","Digoxin acts on which pump? Where?","Digoxin acts on which pump? Where?","Na+/K+-ATPase; Cardiac tissue"]]},{"id":30094,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","s":"A drug activates a Gq-coupled receptor. Which intracellular changes follow first?","o":["PLC activation, then rising IP3 and DAG","Adenylate cyclase activation, then rising cAMP","Adenylate cyclase inhibition, then falling cAMP","Receptor dimerisation and autophosphorylation","Ion pore opening"],"e":"Gαq → phospholipase C → IP3 (↑ intracellular Ca²⁺ from stores) and DAG (activates PKC).","w":["Gαs.","Gαi.","Kinase-linked.","Ion channel."],"k":"Gq: PLC → IP3/DAG."},{"id":30095,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","s":"A drug acting on a Gαs-coupled receptor raises cAMP. What is the second messenger and the next step?","o":["cAMP; activates protein kinase A","Adenylate cyclase; activates PKA","IP3; activates PKC","DAG; releases Ca²⁺","GTP; activates PKA"],"e":"Gαs activates adenylate cyclase (the enzyme), which converts ATP to cAMP (the second messenger); cAMP activates protein kinase A.","w":["The enzyme, not second messenger.","Gq.","DAG activates PKC.","Not."],"k":"Gs: cAMP → PKA.","rn":[["","Gs: enzyme, second messenger, next step?","Gs: enzyme, second messenger, next step?","Activates adenylyl cyclase; cAMP; Protein kinase A"]]},{"id":30096,"t":"Pharmacodynamics and Drug Effects","s":"Which statement about the placebo effect is correct?","o":["A dummy pill that makes someone feel better","An active drug with no side effects, used in clinical trials","A drug with only toxic effects","An inverse agonist at all receptors","A drug that binds irreversibly"],"e":"Placebo: a “fake” or dummy pill or drug (e.g. a sugar tablet) where the person does not know and says “I feel better with this”; used in clinical trials where acceptable.","w":["Placebo is a dummy.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Placebo: dummy pill.","rn":[["","What is the placebo effect?","What is the placebo effect?","Feeling better with a dummy (fake) drug; e.g. a sugar tablet; The person does not know"]]},{"id":30097,"t":"Summary","s":"Which statement about the doctor's role in drug response is correct?","o":["The doctor’s attitude affects response","The doctor’s attitude has no effect, as only the drug acts","Only the drug dose matters","Only genetics matters","Patients’ mood never matters"],"e":"Drug response is influenced by the doctor’s mood, personality and attitude, pharmacogenetics, the patient’s mood and individual factors, correct dose and drug, social environment, and other drugs and diseases.","w":["It does.","Not only.","Not only.","It does."],"k":"Doctor’s attitude matters.","rn":[["","Which factors shape the response to a drug?","Which factors shape the response to a drug?","Pharmacogenetics; Doctor's and patient's mood, personality and attitude; Other drugs and diseases; Social environment"]]},{"id":30098,"t":"Selectivity, Desensitisation and Tolerance","s":"Which statement summarises the purpose of drug designers?","o":["Keep it long and selective enough to act","Create new receptors in the body so the drug can act","Make drugs that bind every receptor","Make drugs the body cannot remove","Avoid any binding"],"e":"The body doesn’t like strange compounds and works to remove them. The designer’s job is to make sure the drug stays long enough, is selective enough and acts enough to give the therapeutic effect before the body removes it.","w":["Drugs create nothing.","Selectivity is the goal.","Not.","Most need binding."],"k":"Design: stay, select, act."},{"id":30099,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"An antihistamine stops allergic itching. Which description of its action is correct?","o":["It holds the H1 receptor in the inactive state","It destroys histamine released from mast cells","It blocks histamine release from mast cells","It activates the H1 receptor fully","It chelates histamine"],"e":"An allergen activates a mast cell to release histamine, which acts on the H1 receptor (itching, swelling, inflammation). The antihistamine (inverse agonist) holds the receptor inactive, so there are no histamine effects.","w":["Not chemical.","Not shown.","Opposite.","Not."],"k":"Antihistamine: H1 inactive.","rn":[["","What does an antihistamine (inverse agonist) do at the histamine H1-receptor?","What does an antihistamine (inverse agonist) do at the histamine H1-receptor?","Keeps the receptor inactive; Stops itching, swelling, inflammation"]]},{"id":30100,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"Which of these drugs acts by a direct physicochemical effect?","o":["A bulk-forming laxative","Omeprazole (proton pump inhibitor)","Thiazide","Aspirin","Metoprolol"],"e":"Direct physicochemical effects: bulk-forming laxatives, antacids, osmotic diuretics – no binding.","w":["It binds an ion pump.","Carrier.","Enzyme.","Receptor."],"k":"Bulk laxative: physicochemical.","rn":[["","What are the 3 examples of a direct physicochemical effect?","What are the 3 examples of a direct physicochemical effect?","Bulk forming laxatives; Antacids; Osmotic diuretics"]]},{"id":30101,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"A drug increases the transcription of an enzyme, giving more enzyme. Where in pharmacology does this happen?","o":["Enzyme induction","Competitive antagonism at the enzyme active site","Physicochemical effects","Tachyphylaxis","Chemical antagonism"],"e":"Increasing genetic transcription of an enzyme is what happens in enzyme induction in drug metabolism: you get many more enzymes (covered by Dr Sadiq).","w":["Induction gives more enzyme.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"More enzyme = induction.","rn":[["","Why are narrow-window drugs dangerous?","Why are narrow-window drugs dangerous?","Small changes push them toxic or sub-therapeutic; e.g. interactions, enzyme induction / inhibition"]]},{"id":30102,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","s":"Which statement about why ligand-gated ion channels don’t all respond to one drug is correct?","o":["They are specific (nicotine ≠ noradrenaline)","They are all identical, so one drug affects every channel","They only open with voltage","They lack subunits","They all bind every ligand"],"e":"Drugs do not affect all channels: some heartbeat control is ligand-gated, some voltage-gated, and ligand-gated channels are specific – nicotine’s receptor will not bind noradrenaline.","w":["They are specific.","That is voltage-gated.","Five subunits.","Not."],"k":"Ligand-gated channels are specific."},{"id":30103,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","s":"Which statement about GPCR structure is correct?","o":["7 passes plus an αβγ G protein","5 subunits forming an ion pore that opens on binding","A single pass with a tyrosine kinase","A nuclear protein binding DNA","A carrier for Na⁺ and Cl⁻"],"e":"The receptor is the transmembrane part (the “little worm”, 7 passes) plus the G protein – hence G protein-coupled. The G protein has three subunits: α, β, γ.","w":["That is an ion channel.","Kinase-linked.","Nuclear.","Carrier."],"k":"GPCR structure.","rn":[["","What is the full G protein-coupled receptor made of?","What is the full G protein-coupled receptor made of?","7-transmembrane part; Plus the G protein"]]},{"id":30104,"t":"Major Types of Receptors","s":"What happens to α after it activates its target?","o":["It hydrolyses GTP to GDP and returns to rest","It phosphorylates the receptor permanently","It enters the nucleus","It becomes the second messenger","It stays active indefinitely"],"e":"α has autocatalytic activity: it converts GTP back to GDP, so activation stops and the receptor returns to rest. (What happens to released GDP was not known.)","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"α: GTP → GDP, off.","rn":[["","How does the GPCR signal stop?","How does the GPCR signal stop?","Alpha hydrolyses GTP to GDP; Autocatalytic activity"]]},{"id":30105,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"A drug shifts an agonist curve to the right without changing the maximum. Which statement about the antagonist is correct?","o":["It is surmountable, so more agonist restores the full effect","It is irreversible, so the maximum can never be reached","It acts at an allosteric site","It has zero affinity","It is a partial agonist"],"e":"A parallel right shift with unchanged maximum is competitive reversible (surmountable) antagonism; adding agonist can still reach 100%.","w":["Max falls if irreversible.","Max falls.","It needs affinity.","Not."],"k":"Right shift = surmountable.","rn":[["","Competitive reversible antagonist: effect on the agonist curve?","Competitive reversible antagonist: effect on the agonist curve?","Shifts right; Same maximal response"],["","Image card: Agonist curves with rising antagonist concentration. What type of antagonist is this? H…","Agonist curves with rising antagonist concentration. What type of antagonist is this? How can you tell?","Competitive reversible; Curve shifts right; Maximal response unchanged (surmountable)"]]},{"id":30106,"t":"Individual Drug Response","s":"Which drug interaction affects codeine's metabolism?","o":["Rifampicin raising its metabolism","Antacids increasing codeine absorption in the stomach","Naloxone increasing codeine effect","Aspirin binding codeine chemically","Thiazides increasing codeine excretion"],"e":"Interactions between drugs or with food: e.g. codeine with rifampicin (name as heard), which increases metabolism, changing codeine’s effect. Compounds can also react chemically in the stomach so neither is absorbed.","w":["Not given.","Antagonist.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Codeine + rifampicin."},{"id":30107,"t":"Individual Drug Response","s":"Which statement about sex and alcohol as a factor in drug response was made?","o":["Women are less tolerant (enzymatic)","Men are less tolerant to alcohol due to body composition","Sex never affects drug response","Alcohol only affects excretion","Alcohol increases receptor numbers"],"e":"Alcohol affects metabolism, and sex matters: e.g. women are less tolerant to alcohol (enzymatic).","w":["Women were named.","It does.","Metabolism.","Not."],"k":"Sex/alcohol: enzymatic.","rn":[["","Why are women less tolerant to alcohol than men?","Why are women less tolerant to alcohol than men?","Enzymatic differences (metabolism)"]]},{"id":30108,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Which statement is FALSE?","o":["A full agonist reaches maximal response only when all its receptors are occupied","The EC50 is the concentration producing a half-maximal response","Changes in receptor numbers can cause tolerance","A competitive antagonist shifts the curve right without changing the maximum","A partial agonist cannot reach the full agonist’s maximum"],"e":"A full agonist does not need to occupy all receptors to give the full response (the favourite cake: every bite is happiness). The others are true.","w":["True.","True.","True.","True."],"k":"Full agonist needs not all receptors.","rn":[["","Does a full agonist need to occupy all receptors for full response?","Does a full agonist need to occupy all receptors for full response?","No"]]},{"id":30109,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"A drug has high affinity but zero efficacy. What is it?","o":["An antagonist","A full agonist","A partial agonist","A drug with no receptor","A physicochemical drug"],"e":"Antagonists bind (affinity) but have zero efficacy – no regulatory effect – and prevent the endogenous compound binding.","w":["Efficacy 10.","Some efficacy.","It binds.","No binding."],"k":"Affinity, no efficacy = antagonist.","rn":[["","What is Antagonist?","What is Antagonist?","Binds but has no regulatory effect; Prevents binding of endogenous compounds"],["","Is this true: an antagonist has zero affinity?","Is this true: an antagonist has zero affinity?","False; Antagonists need affinity"]]},{"id":30110,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"A drug with a narrow therapeutic window needs which approach to keep levels safe?","e":"Therapeutic drug monitoring keeps plasma concentration between sub-therapeutic and toxic levels.","k":"Therapeutic drug monitoring.","a":["therapeutic drug monitoring","tdm","drug level monitoring","monitoring"],"rn":[["","What do narrow therapeutic window drugs need?","What do narrow therapeutic window drugs need?","Therapeutic drug monitoring"]]},{"id":30111,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"In the repeated-dose graph, what must stay between the supratherapeutic and sub-therapeutic lines?","o":["Plasma concentration","The receptor number in the target tissue","The EC50","The AUC of one dose","The t max"],"e":"With repeated doses the concentration rises and falls with each dose and must stay between the supratherapeutic and sub-therapeutic limits over time; with a narrow window this needs care each time.","w":["Concentration must stay in range.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Keep levels in the window.","rn":[["","What does a plasma concentration curve for a double dose show?","What does a plasma concentration curve for a double dose show?","Higher Cmax (highest concentration); Same tmax (time to Cmax)"],["","What is half-life (t1/2)?","What is half-life (t1/2)?","Time for plasma drug concentration to halve"]]},{"id":30112,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Which statement about drug–receptor interactions is correct?","o":["Multiple, complex, not 100% specific","Always one drug to one receptor, at every dose","They are fully specific at all doses","They never vary between people","They create new functions"],"e":"Key messages: all drugs have multiple effects which differ individually; drug–receptor interactions are multiple, complex and not 100% specific; understanding them explains mode of action and effects; PD and PK matter for safer prescribing.","w":["Not 100% specific.","Not.","They vary.","Not."],"k":"Interactions: not 100% specific."},{"id":30113,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"What does “surmountable” mean?","o":["Antagonism overcome by adding more agonist","Antagonism that lowers maximum response","An agonist that occupies all receptors","A drug acting without binding","A drug with a wide window"],"e":"Surmountable antagonism is overcome by more agonist (competitive reversible). Irreversible and allosteric antagonism is unsurmountable – maximum falls.","w":["Unsurmountable.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Surmountable definition.","rn":[["","How is competitive reversible antagonism overcome?","How is competitive reversible antagonism overcome?","Add more agonist"]]},{"id":30114,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Which statement about the two-state receptor model is correct?","o":["Receptors switch; inverse agonists keep more at rest","Receptors are fixed in one state until a drug binds","Inverse agonists destroy active receptors","Antagonists move all receptors to R*","Agonists bind only resting receptors"],"e":"The receptor constantly switches between active and resting (an equilibrium); an inverse agonist changes that balance so more receptors stay at rest. An agonist binds the active state; an antagonist binds both.","w":["They switch constantly.","Not.","No shift.","Active state."],"k":"Equilibrium; inverse shifts to rest.","rn":[["","Two-state model: which state does each ligand bind?","Two-state model: which state does each ligand bind?","Agonist: active state; Inverse agonist: resting state; Neutral antagonist: both"],["","What does an antihistamine (inverse agonist) do at the histamine H1-receptor?","What does an antihistamine (inverse agonist) do at the histamine H1-receptor?","Keeps the receptor inactive; Stops itching, swelling, inflammation"]]},{"id":30115,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Name a competitive reversible antagonist used at α-adrenoceptors.","e":"Prazosin is a competitive reversible α1 antagonist.","k":"Prazosin.","a":["prazosin","doxazosin"],"rn":[["","Competitive reversible antagonists: 4 examples?","Competitive reversible antagonists: 4 examples?","β-blockers; Atropine; Naloxone; Prazosin"]]},{"id":30116,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"Atropine and β-blockers share which property?","o":["Competitive reversible","Irreversible antagonism at the same site","Full agonism","Inverse agonism","Chemical antagonism"],"e":"β-blockers, atropine, naloxone and prazosin are listed as competitive reversible antagonists.","w":["They are reversible.","Morphine.","Pimavanserin.","Dimercaprol."],"k":"Atropine, β-blockers: competitive.","rn":[["","Competitive reversible antagonists: 4 examples?","Competitive reversible antagonists: 4 examples?","β-blockers; Atropine; Naloxone; Prazosin"]]},{"id":30117,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"Why must care be taken when warfarin is co-prescribed with CYP450 inducers?","o":["Care with CP450 inducers","It is a direct physicochemical drug acting in the gut","It is an inverse agonist","It acts on GPCRs only","It has no interactions"],"e":"With warfarin you must be careful if the patient takes something that induces CP450, so you need to know what induction means. The dose-response and therapeutic window concepts matter especially in HLB.","w":["Inducers matter for warfarin.","Not stated.","Not stated.","It does."],"k":"Warfarin + inducer."},{"id":30118,"t":"Summary","s":"Which pairing of drug target and example drug is correct?","o":["Carrier – thiazides","Ion pump – thiazide diuretics in the renal tubule","Enzyme – omeprazole","Physicochemical – digoxin","Ion pump – antacids"],"e":"Carrier (symport): thiazides. Ion pumps: omeprazole and digoxin. Enzymes: cyclooxygenase (aspirin/ibuprofen), acetylcholinesterase. Physicochemical: antacids, laxatives, osmotic diuretics.","w":["Thiazides block a carrier (symport).","Pump.","Pump.","Physicochemical."],"k":"Targets and examples.","rn":[["","Most drug targets are what? Name the 4.","Most drug targets are what? Name the 4.","Proteins; Receptors; Enzymes; Ion channels; Carriers"]]},{"id":30119,"t":"Selectivity, Desensitisation and Tolerance","s":"Which statement about drugs and receptor number changes is correct?","o":["Agonists: receptor removal; blockers: more receptors","Agonists always add receptors; blockers always remove them from the membrane","Receptor number never changes","Only antagonists cause tolerance","Only enzymes change"],"e":"With an agonist the body may remove receptors from the membrane (translocation), so response falls; with a blocker the body may make more receptors. Both cause tolerance.","w":["Reversed.","It changes.","Both.","Not."],"k":"Receptor regulation.","rn":[["","Long-term β-blocker: what does the body do?","Long-term β-blocker: what does the body do?","Makes more receptors; Tolerance; need higher dose"]]},{"id":30120,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"Which best summarises why pharmacodynamics and pharmacokinetics matter for prescribing?","o":["Dose-response curves and windows for safety","They replace the need to know side effects once the dose is right","They mean all drugs work the same in all patients","They are only for researchers","They decide brand names"],"e":"PD and PK data allow dose-response curves: C max, t max, t½, AUC, ED50/TD50/LD50, TI and the therapeutic window – for safe prescribing.","w":["No drug is free of side effects.","Response varies.","For clinicians.","Not."],"k":"PD/PK → safe prescribing.","rn":[["","What do dose-response curves quantify?","What do dose-response curves quantify?","The pharmacodynamics of a drug"]]},{"id":30121,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"In an organ bath, a researcher builds a log dose–response curve for an agonist. She then adds a fixed amount of drug X and repeats the curve: the curve moves to the right, but at high agonist doses the same maximum response is still reached. With drug Y instead, the maximum response is permanently lower however much agonist is added.<br><br>What kinds of antagonist are X and Y?","o":["X: competitive reversible (surmountable); Y: irreversible or noncompetitive (unsurmountable)","X: irreversible; Y: competitive reversible","X: partial agonist; Y: full agonist","X: inverse agonist; Y: chemical antagonist","Both competitive reversible, because both bind the receptor and shift the dose–response curve"],"e":"Step 1: a competitive reversible antagonist binds and unbinds at the same (orthosteric) site, so adding more agonist overcomes it: the curve shifts right with the maximum response unchanged (surmountable). Step 2: irreversible antagonists, or noncompetitive allosteric ones binding a different site, are unsurmountable, so the maximum response falls.","w":["The two descriptions are swapped.","Partial and full agonists produce responses on their own.","An inverse agonist reduces constitutive activity, and a chemical antagonist inactivates the agonist.","Only X keeps the same maximum; Y lowers it."],"k":"Competitive reversible: right shift, same Rmax; irreversible/allosteric: Rmax ↓.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of receptor antagonist?","What are the 3 types of receptor antagonist?","Competitive reversible; Irreversible; Noncompetitive (allosteric)"],["","How is antagonist type decided by binding site and reversibility?","How is antagonist type decided by binding site and reversibility?","Active site, reversible: competitive; Active site, irreversible: noncompetitive; Allosteric site: noncompetitive"],["","Image card: Agonist curves with rising antagonist concentration. What type of antagonist is this? H…","Agonist curves with rising antagonist concentration. What type of antagonist is this? How can you tell?","Competitive reversible; Curve shifts right; Maximal response unchanged (surmountable)"]]},{"id":30122,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"A patient with severe pain is given morphine, which gives strong pain relief. Another patient is given buprenorphine; even at its highest dose, the response never reaches what morphine can achieve, although it binds the same receptors. When both are present, buprenorphine reduces the effect of morphine.<br><br>Which statement best explains these observations?","o":["Morphine is a full agonist; buprenorphine is a partial agonist with lower efficacy","Morphine is a partial agonist; buprenorphine is a full agonist","Buprenorphine has zero affinity for the receptor","Buprenorphine is an inverse agonist that only works at constitutively active receptors in the brain","Both are antagonists with zero efficacy"],"e":"Step 1: a full agonist (e.g. morphine) gives a full signal and does not need to occupy all receptors, while a partial agonist (e.g. buprenorphine) produces a response but cannot reach the maximum even when binding all receptors. Step 2: because buprenorphine binds the same receptors (it has affinity) but has lower efficacy, it occupies receptors that morphine would otherwise use, reducing morphine's effect.","w":["The roles are swapped.","Buprenorphine must bind (have affinity) to have any effect.","Buprenorphine is described as a partial agonist, not an inverse agonist.","Both produce responses, so neither has zero efficacy."],"k":"Full agonist: max response; partial agonist: lower efficacy, can reduce full agonist's effect.","rn":[["","Efficacy on 0-10: full agonist, partial agonist, antagonist?","Efficacy on 0-10: full agonist, partial agonist, antagonist?","Full: 10; Partial: between 0 and 10; Antagonist: 0"],["","Buprenorphine is an example of what?","Buprenorphine is an example of what?","Partial agonist"],["","Morphine is an example of what?","Morphine is an example of what?","Full agonist"]]},{"id":30123,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"Two drugs are compared. Drug A has a TD50 of 400 mg and an ED50 of 10 mg. Drug B has a TD50 of 30 mg and an ED50 of 15 mg. The hospital decides that one of them needs regular blood level checks during treatment.<br><br>Which drug needs monitoring, and why?","o":["Drug B: TI = 2, a narrow therapeutic window","Drug A: TI = 40, a narrow therapeutic window","Drug B: TI = 450, a wide window","Drug A: its ED50 is lower, so it is more toxic","Neither, because the therapeutic index has no bearing on whether blood levels need to be monitored"],"e":"Step 1: the therapeutic index is TI = TD50 / ED50, so drug A has TI = 400/10 = 40 and drug B has TI = 30/15 = 2. Step 2: a large TI gives a wide therapeutic window (safer), while a small TI gives a narrow window, so therapeutic drug monitoring is needed for drug B.","w":["Drug A has a large TI and a wide window.","Drug B's TI is 2, not 450.","A lower ED50 means potency, not toxicity.","A small TI means a narrow window needing monitoring."],"k":"TI = TD50/ED50; small TI → narrow window → monitor.","rn":[["","What do narrow therapeutic window drugs need?","What do narrow therapeutic window drugs need?","Therapeutic drug monitoring"],["","Large TI vs small TI?","Large TI vs small TI?","Large: wide therapeutic window; Small: narrow therapeutic window"]]},{"id":30124,"t":"Dose-Response and Therapeutic Window","s":"A researcher plots log dose–response curves for two agonists acting on the same receptor. Drug P's curve lies to the left of drug Q's, but both reach the same maximum response. A student concludes that drug Q is more potent because its curve is further right.<br><br>What is the correct interpretation?","o":["P is more potent (lower EC50); both have the same efficacy (Rmax)","Q is more potent because its curve is further right","P has higher efficacy because its curve is to the left","Q has higher affinity but zero efficacy, which makes it a competitive antagonist of P at the receptor","They have equal potency because Rmax is the same"],"e":"Step 1: potency is the amount of drug needed for an effect of given intensity, measured by EC50; a curve further right needs more drug for the same effect and is less potent. Step 2: efficacy is how effective the drug is (Rmax); since both reach the same maximum, they have the same efficacy.","w":["A curve further right means less potent.","Curve position shows potency, not efficacy.","Q produces a response, so it is an agonist.","Same Rmax means same efficacy, not same potency."],"k":"Left curve = more potent (lower EC50); Rmax = efficacy.","rn":[["","Lower EC50 means what?","Lower EC50 means what?","More potent"]]},{"id":30125,"t":"Selectivity, Desensitisation and Tolerance","s":"A 60-year-old man with asthma and high blood pressure is given propranolol, and his asthma worsens. His doctor switches him to metoprolol, and his breathing improves while his heart rate is still controlled.<br><br>Why did the switch help, and what caveat applies?","o":["Metoprolol is β1-selective; propranolol blocks β1 and β2 equally; selectivity is never perfect and depends on dose","Propranolol is β1-selective; metoprolol blocks β2 only","Metoprolol is a β2-agonist like salbutamol","Propranolol is an inverse agonist of 5-HT2A receptors","Metoprolol is completely selective at every dose, so it can never affect β2 receptors in the lungs"],"e":"Step 1: drug selectivity is a drug's ability to bind a particular receptor type or subtype; propranolol (a β-blocker) binds equally to β1- and β2-adrenoceptors, while metoprolol (cardioselective) binds selectively to β1. Step 2: no drug is completely selective, and selectivity might depend on dose.","w":["Propranolol is non-selective.","Metoprolol is a β-blocker, not an agonist.","Pimavanserin, not propranolol, is the 5-HT2A inverse agonist.","No drug is completely selective."],"k":"Propranolol non-selective; metoprolol β1-selective; selectivity never perfect.","rn":[["","Is any drug completely selective?","Is any drug completely selective?","No; Depends on dose"],["","Propranolol binds which adrenoceptors?","Propranolol binds which adrenoceptors?","β1 and β2 equally"]]},{"id":30126,"t":"Selectivity, Desensitisation and Tolerance","s":"Patient A receives repeated doses of a drug over a few minutes, and its effect quickly fades. Patient B takes a benzodiazepine nightly; over several weeks he needs higher doses to get the same effect. Studies show patient B's target cells have fewer receptors on their surface.<br><br>Which processes are these?","o":["A: desensitisation (tachyphylaxis); B: tolerance from receptor down-regulation","A: tolerance; B: desensitisation","Both tolerance over minutes","A: inverse agonism; B: competitive antagonism","A: tolerance from receptor translocation; B: tachyphylaxis, which develops over weeks of use"],"e":"Step 1: desensitisation or tachyphylaxis is when a drug's effect gradually diminishes with continuous or repeated dosing over minutes. Step 2: tolerance is a more gradual decrease in responsiveness over hours, days or weeks (e.g. benzodiazepines), and changes in receptor numbers (up- and down-regulation, translocation) can cause it.","w":["The two are swapped.","Tolerance develops over hours to weeks, not minutes.","These are not antagonism or inverse agonism.","Tachyphylaxis is over minutes, tolerance over weeks."],"k":"Tachyphylaxis: minutes; tolerance: hours–weeks (receptor changes).","rn":[["","What is desensitisation (tachyphylaxis)?","What is desensitisation (tachyphylaxis)?","Effect gradually diminishes with continuous or repeated dosing; Over minutes"],["","Which receptors show up- and down-regulation?","Which receptors show up- and down-regulation?","β-adrenoceptors"]]},{"id":30127,"t":"Molecular Mechanisms of Action","s":"A student groups four drugs by their target. Omeprazole acts on a pump in gastric parietal cells. Digoxin acts on a pump in cardiac tissue. Thiazide diuretics act on a co-transporter in the renal tubule. An antacid works without binding any target, by neutralising acid directly.<br><br>Which pairing of drug and mechanism is correct?","o":["Omeprazole: K⁺/H⁺-ATPase; digoxin: Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase; thiazides: Na⁺/Cl⁻ symport; antacid: direct physicochemical effect","Omeprazole: Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase; digoxin: K⁺/H⁺-ATPase; thiazides: GPCR; antacid: enzyme","Omeprazole: GPCR; digoxin: ion channel; thiazides: antiport; antacid: receptor","All four: GPCRs","Omeprazole: K⁺/H⁺-ATPase; digoxin: Na⁺/Cl⁻ symport; thiazides: Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase; antacid: an enzyme"],"e":"Step 1: transport-system targets include ion pumps (omeprazole on the K⁺/H⁺-ATPase in gastric parietal cells; digoxin on the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase in cardiac tissue) and carrier proteins (thiazides blocking Na⁺/Cl⁻ co-transport, a symport, in the renal tubule). Step 2: a direct physicochemical effect, such as an antacid, bulk-forming laxative or osmotic diuretic, works without binding a target.","w":["The pumps for omeprazole and digoxin are swapped.","These drugs act on transport systems, not GPCRs.","These are not GPCR drugs.","Digoxin and thiazide targets are swapped, and antacids need no target."],"k":"Omeprazole K⁺/H⁺-ATPase; digoxin Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase; thiazide Na⁺/Cl⁻ symport.","rn":[["","What are the 3 examples of a direct physicochemical effect?","What are the 3 examples of a direct physicochemical effect?","Bulk forming laxatives; Antacids; Osmotic diuretics"],["","What are the 4 molecular mechanisms of drug action?","What are the 4 molecular mechanisms of drug action?","Direct physicochemical effect; Transport systems; Enzymes; The 4 major types of receptors"],["","Digoxin acts on which pump? Where?","Digoxin acts on which pump? Where?","Na+/K+-ATPase; Cardiac tissue"]]},{"id":30128,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"A child swallows a heavy metal such as arsenic and is treated with dimercaprol, which binds the metal itself so it cannot act. Separately, a patient whose blood pressure has fallen because of histamine is treated with adrenaline, which acts at a different receptor to raise pressure.<br><br>Which types of antagonism are these?","o":["Dimercaprol: chemical; adrenaline vs histamine: functional (physiological)","Dimercaprol: competitive; adrenaline: irreversible","Both chemical","Dimercaprol: functional; adrenaline: chemical","Both competitive reversible, because both drugs block the same receptors as the agonist"],"e":"Step 1: chemical antagonism is when a drug binds to and inactivates the agonist itself rather than the receptor, as with dimercaprol (a chelating agent) and heavy metals. Step 2: functional (physiological) antagonism is when drugs have opposing physiological actions at different receptors, as with histamine and adrenaline on arterial pressure.","w":["Dimercaprol binds the metal, not the receptor.","Adrenaline acts at a different receptor.","The labels are swapped.","Neither acts at the same receptor as the agonist."],"k":"Chemical: inactivates agonist; functional: opposing actions at different receptors.","rn":[["","Example of functional antagonism?","Example of functional antagonism?","Histamine and adrenaline on arterial pressure"],["","What is functional (physiological) antagonism?","What is functional (physiological) antagonism?","Opposing physiological actions; At different receptors"]]},{"id":30129,"t":"Agonists and Antagonists","s":"A receptor in a patient's brain shows background activity even when no agonist is present. A drug is given that binds these receptors and reduces their activity below this background level. The drug is used as an antipsychotic.<br><br>What type of drug is this, and which concept explains the background activity?","o":["An inverse agonist; constitutive activity (two-state model)","A full agonist; desensitisation","A competitive antagonist; tolerance","A partial agonist; chemical antagonism","A noncompetitive antagonist; receptor down-regulation, which explains why the receptor is active without agonist"],"e":"Step 1: some receptors have background activity without an agonist (constitutively active receptors, e.g. histamine and some GABA receptors), explained by the two-state model of active and inactive states. Step 2: inverse agonists bind active receptors and move them to the inactive state (negative activity), e.g. pimavanserin at the 5-HT2A receptor as an antipsychotic.","w":["A full agonist increases activity.","A competitive antagonist blocks agonists but does not reduce constitutive activity below baseline.","A partial agonist produces some positive activity.","Down-regulation is a tolerance mechanism."],"k":"Inverse agonist: reduces constitutive activity (two-state model).","rn":[["","Two-state model: what 2 states can receptors be in?","Two-state model: what 2 states can receptors be in?","Active (constitutive activity); Inactive (resting)"],["","What is Inverse agonist?","What is Inverse agonist?","Binds active receptors; Moves them to the inactive state; Negative activity"],["","Two-state model: which state does each ligand bind?","Two-state model: which state does each ligand bind?","Agonist: active state; Inverse agonist: resting state; Neutral antagonist: both"]]},{"id":30130,"t":"Summary","s":"A patient takes a normal dose of a drug and gets the intended benefit but also a dry mouth. A second patient on a normal dose has a harmful reaction. A third patient takes a large overdose and is poisoned.<br><br>Which terms describe these three effects?","o":["Side effect (umbrella); adverse effect; toxic effect","Toxic effect; therapeutic effect; adverse effect","Adverse effect (umbrella); side effect; therapeutic effect","Therapeutic effect; toxic effect; side effect","All three are toxic effects, as any unwanted effect means the dose was too high"],"e":"Step 1: a side effect is an undesirable secondary effect occurring with the therapeutic effect at a normal dose (an umbrella term that includes adverse effects), and an adverse effect is a harmful reaction under normal conditions of use. Step 2: a toxic effect is a deleterious effect after a higher dose, as in drug poisoning.","w":["Dry mouth at a normal dose is a side effect, not toxic.","Side effect is the umbrella term, not adverse effect.","The terms are mismatched.","Toxic effects follow a higher dose; the first two were at normal doses."],"k":"Side (umbrella, normal dose), adverse (harmful, normal use), toxic (high dose).","rn":[["","Is this true: an adverse effect includes side and toxic effects?","Is this true: an adverse effect includes side and toxic effects?","False; Side effects are the umbrella term"],["","Which effect is the \"umbrella\" term?","Which effect is the \"umbrella\" term?","Side effects; Includes adverse effects"],["","What is an adverse effect?","What is an adverse effect?","Harmful reaction; Under normal conditions of use; A type of side effect"]]}]);
